(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_organismsandpopulations=[{id:"neet-2026-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Sexual deceit in pollination",q:"Which one of the following is an appropriate example of sexual deceit?",o:["Female wasp and fig","Cuckoo and crow","Ophrys and bumblebee","Sea anemone and clown fish"],c:2,s:`Female wasp and fig is mutualism.
Ophrys and bumblebee is sexual deceit.
Sea anemone and clown fish is commensalism.
Cuckoo and crow is brood parasitism.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q159",pn:159,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Both species are harmed \u2014 I. Predation
B. One species is harmed and the other is benefited \u2014 II. Mutualism
C. Both species are benefited \u2014 III. Competition
D. One is benefited while the other has no effect \u2014 IV. Commensalism
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],c:2,s:`Both species are harmed in competition.
One species is harmed and the other benefited in predation.
Both species are benefited in mutualism.
One species is benefited while the other has no effect in commensalism.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q162",pn:162,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competition",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced.
Reason R : Goats were more efficient at browsing than Abingdon tortoise.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced there.
This was apparently due to the greater browsing efficiency of the goats.`},{id:"neet-2026-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic population growth",q:"Which of the following equations depicts Verhulst-Pearl logistic population growth?",o:["dN/dt = rN((K \u2212 N)/K)","dN/dt = rN((K + N)/K)","dN/dt = rN(K/(K \u2212 N))","dN/dt = rN((K \u2212 N)/N)"],c:0,s:`Verhulst-Pearl logistic growth is given by dN/dt = rN((K \u2212 N)/K).
Here K = carrying capacity.
r = intrinsic rate of natural increase.
N = population density at time t.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q175",pn:175,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth models",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: The logistic growth model of populations is considered more realistic than the exponential growth model.
Reason R: Resources are finite.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`The logistic model is more realistic than the exponential model because resources in nature are limited (finite).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q177",pn:177,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth models",q:"Consider a population of 10 million cells. Given the per-capita birth rate of 0.002 (per unit time) and the per-capita death rate of 0.002 (per unit time), the expected number of cells after 10 generations is ______.",o:["5 million","10 million","100 million","1 million"],c:1,s:`Per-capita birth rate b = 0.002.
Per-capita death rate d = 0.002.
The population equation is dN/dt = (b \u2212 d)N.
r = (b \u2212 d) = 0.002 \u2212 0.002 = 0.
So dN/dt = 0.
The population stays constant, so after 10 generations it is still 10 million.`},{id:"neet-2026-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:`Choose the correct statements regarding population interactions between two species.
A. In both parasitism and commensalism, only one species benefits and the other species is harmed.
B. Both species benefit in mutualism.
C. Both species benefit in commensalism.
D. In parasitism, only one species benefits and the other species is harmed.
E. In amensalism, one species is harmed and the other is unaffected.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and D only","A and B only","B and E only","B, D and E only"],c:3,s:`In parasitism, one species benefits and the other is harmed.
In commensalism, one species benefits and the other remains unaffected.
So A and C are wrong; B, D and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2025-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions, commensalism",q:"Epiphytes that are growing on a mango branch is an example of which of the following?",o:["Commensalism","Mutualism","Predation","Amensalism"],c:0,s:`Commensalism is the interaction in which one species benefits and the other is neither harmed nor benefited.
An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch is an example of commensalism.`},{id:"neet-2025-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Ecology: history",q:"Who is known as the father of Ecology in India?",o:["S.R. Kashyap","Ramdeo Misra","Ram Udar","Birbal Sahni"],c:1,s:"Ramdeo Misra is known as the father of Ecology in India."},{id:"neet-2025-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Mutualism",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Fig fruit is a non-vegetarian fruit as it has enclosed fig wasps in it.
Statement II: Fig wasp and fig tree exhibit mutual relationship as fig wasp completes its life cycle in fig fruit and fig fruit gets pollinated by fig wasp.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:1,s:`Fig fruit is a vegetarian fruit. The fig is pollinated by the wasp.
Fig tree and fig wasp show mutualism, in which both species benefit. So statement I is incorrect.
Statement II is also not correct, because the fig inflorescence/flower gets pollinated by the fig wasp, not the fruit.`},{id:"neet-2025-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic growth equation",q:"Which one of the following equations represents the Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth of population?",o:["dN/dt = r((K \u2013 N)/K)","dN/dt = rN((K \u2013 N)/K)","dN/dt = rN((N \u2013 K)/N)","dN/dt = N((r \u2013 K)/K)"],c:1,s:"Logistic growth is described by the Verhulst-Pearl equation dN/dt = rN((K \u2013 N)/K)."},{id:"neet-2024-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic growth",q:`The equation of Verhulst-Pearl logistic growth is dN/dt = rN[(K \u2013 N)/(K)].
From this equation, K indicates :`,o:["Carrying capacity","Population density","Intrinsic rate of natural increase","Biotic potential"],c:0,s:"In dN/dt = rN[(K \u2013 N)/(K)], K represents the carrying capacity."},{id:"neet-2024-q198",pn:198,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Species interactions",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : Gause's competitive exclusive principle states that two closely related species competing for different resources cannot exist indefinitely.
Statement II : According to Gause's principle, during competition, the inferior will be eliminated. This may be true if resources are limiting.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Both statement I and Statement II are true.","Both statement I and Statement II are false."],c:1,s:`Gause's competitive exclusion principle: two closely related species competing for the SAME resources cannot coexist indefinitely.
The competitively inferior one is eventually eliminated. This may be true if resources are limiting.
Statement I says 'different resources', so it is false; Statement II is true.`},{id:"neet-2023-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:`Match List I with List II :
List I (Interaction): A. Mutualism; B. Commensalism; C. Amensalism; D. Parasitism
List II (Species A and B): I. + (A), 0(B); II. \u2013 (A), 0(B); III. + (A), \u2013 (B); IV. + (A), + (B)
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],c:2,s:`Mutualism (+, +): both benefit. A-IV.
Commensalism (+, 0): one benefits, the other is unaffected. B-I.
Amensalism (\u2013, 0): one is harmed, the other is unaffected. C-II.
Parasitism (+, \u2013): one benefits, the other is harmed. D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competition between species",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : Gause\u2019s \u2018Competitive Exclusion Principle\u2019 states that two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually.
Statement II : In general, carnivores are more adversely affected by competition than herbivores.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is correct Statement II is false."],c:3,s:`Statement I is the correct Competitive Exclusion Principle.
Statement II is wrong: herbivores and plants are generally more affected by competition than carnivores.`},{id:"neet-2023-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I (Interacting species): A. A Leopard and a Lion in a forest/grassland; B. A Cuckoo laying egg in a Crow's nest; C. Fungi and root of a higher plant in Mycorrhizae; D. A cattle egret and Cattle in a field
List II (Name of Interaction): I. Competition; II. Brood parasitism; III. Mutualism; IV. Commensalism
Choose the correct answer from the option given below :`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],c:1,s:`Leopard and lion compete for the same resources: competition (A-I).
Cuckoo lays its egg in the crow's nest, so the crow is the host: brood parasitism (B-II).
Mycorrhiza benefits both: the fungus helps the plant absorb nutrients and the plant gives it carbohydrates: mutualism (C-III).
Cattle egret feeds on insects flushed out by grazing cattle: one gains, the other is unaffected, so commensalism (D-IV).`},{id:"neet-2023-q195",pn:195,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth and age pyramids",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I: A. Logistic growth; B. Exponential growth; C. Expanding age pyramid; D. Stable age pyramid
List II: I. Unlimited resource availability condition; II. Limited resource availability condition; III. The percent individuals of pre-reproductive age is largest followed by reproductive and post reproductive age groups; IV. The percent individuals of pre-reproductives and reproductive age group are same
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],c:1,s:`Logistic growth happens when resources are limited, so A-II.
Exponential growth happens when resources are unlimited, so B-I.
An expanding pyramid has the most pre-reproductive individuals, then reproductive, then post-reproductive, so C-III.
A stable pyramid has pre-reproductive and reproductive groups about equal, so D-IV.`},{id:"neet-2022-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Predation",q:"Which one of the following statements cannot be connected to Predation?",o:["It might lead to extinction of a species","Both the interacting species are negatively impacted","It is necessitated by nature to maintain the ecological balance","It helps in maintaining species diversity in a community"],c:1,s:`In predation one species gains at the cost of the other.
Predators keep diversity by reducing competition among prey.
An over-efficient predator can drive prey extinct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"While explaining interspecific interaction of population, (+) sign is assigned for beneficial interaction, (\u2013) sign is assigned for detrimental interaction and (0) for neutral interaction. Which of the following interactions can be assigned (+) for one specifies and (\u2013) for another specifies involved in the interaction ?",o:["Amensalim","Commensalism","Competition","Predation"],c:3,s:`In predation one species gains and the other is harmed.
So it is a (+ \u2013) interaction.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q147",pn:147,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Niche specialisation in tropics",q:"Frugivorous birds are found in large numbers in tropical forests mainly because of :",o:["temperature conducive for their breeding","lack of niche specialisation","higher annual rainfall","availability of fruits throughout the year"],c:3,s:`Tropical climates are less seasonal and more predictable.
This favours niche specialisation and more species.
High solar energy gives high productivity, so fruits are available all year for frugivores.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q173",pn:173,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competition between species",q:"Two butterfly species are competing for the same nectar of a flower in a garden. To survive and coexist together, they may avoid competition in the same garden by",o:["predating on each other","feeding at the same time","choosing different foraging patterns","increasing time spent on attacking each other"],c:2,s:"Competing species can live together by choosing different foraging patterns."},{id:"neet-2022-q185",pn:185,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population attributes",q:"If \u20188\u2019 Drosophila in a laboratory population of \u201880\u2019 died during a week, the death rate in the population is ______ individuals per Drosophila per week.",o:["10","1.0","Zero","0.1"],c:3,s:`Death rate = deaths / population size.
8 / 80 = 0.1 individuals per Drosophila per week.`},{id:"neet-2021-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Interspecific competition",q:"Inspite of interspecific competition in nature, which mechanism the competing species might have evolved for their survival ?",o:["Predation","Resource partitioning","Competitive release","Mutualism"],c:1,s:`Competing species can survive together by using the resource differently, for example feeding at different times or on different parts. This is resource partitioning.
Competitive release is a rise in one species when its competitor is removed.`},{id:"neet-2021-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"Amensalism can be represented as:",o:["Species A (+); Species B (0)","Species A (\u2013); Species B (0)","Species A (+); Species B (+)","Species A (\u2013); Species B (\u2013)"],c:1,s:`In amensalism one species is harmed while the other is unaffected: (\u2013, 0).
(+, 0) is commensalism, (+, +) is mutualism, (\u2013, \u2013) is competition.`},{id:"neet-2021-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth",q:"In the exponential growth equation Nt = N\u2080 e\u02B3\u1D57, e represents",o:["The base of geometric logarithms","The base of number logarithms","The base of exponential logarithms","The base of natural logarithms"],c:3,s:`In Nt = N\u2080 e^(rt), N\u2080 is the starting density, r the intrinsic rate of increase and t the time.
The constant e (about 2.718) is the base of natural logarithms.`},{id:"neet-2021-q186",pn:186,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Adaptations",q:`Match List-I with List - II
List-I: (a) Allen's Rule (b) Physiological adaptation (c) Behavioural adaptation (d) Biochemical adaptation
List-II: (i) Kangaroo rat (ii) Desert lizard (iii) Marine fish at depth (iv) Polar seal`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:3,s:`Allen's rule: mammals in cold regions have shorter limbs and ears, as in the polar seal: a-iv.
The kangaroo rat copes with desert by physiology (concentrated urine): b-i.
The desert lizard basks and burrows, a behavioural adaptation: c-ii.
Deep-sea fish have biochemical adaptations to depth: d-iii.`},{id:"neet-2020-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population attributes",q:"Which of the following is NOT an attribute of a population ?",o:["Natality","Mortality","Species interaction","Sex ratio"],c:2,s:`Birth rate, death rate and sex ratio are population attributes.
Species interaction belongs to a community.`},{id:"neet-2018-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Niche",q:"Niche is -",o:["All the biological factors in the organism's environment","The physical space where an organism lives","The range of temperature that the organism needs to live","The functional role played by the organism where it lives."],c:3,s:`Habitat is the place where an organism lives.
Niche is the functional role of an organism in its habitat, including its resources and interactions.`},{id:"neet-2018-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population attributes",q:"Natality refers to",o:["Death rate","Birth rate","Number of individuals leaving the habitat","Number of individuals entering a habitat"],c:1,s:`Natality is the number of births in a population in a given time, so it is the birth rate.
Mortality is death rate, emigration is leaving and immigration is entering.`},{id:"neet-2018-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Mutualism",q:"Which one of the following plants shows a very close relationship with a species of moth, where none of the two can complete its life cycle without the other ?",o:["Hydrilla","Yucca","Banana","Viola"],c:1,s:`Yucca and the Pronuba moth are mutualists.
The moth pollinates the flower and lays eggs in the ovary, and the larvae feed on some seeds.
Neither can finish its life cycle without the other.`},{id:"neet-2018-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Age pyramids",q:"In a growing population of a country,",o:["pre-reproductive individuals are more than the reproductive individuals.","reproductive individuals are less than the post-reproductive individuals.","reproductive and pre-reproductive individuals are equal in number.","pre-reproductive individuals are less than the reproductive individuals."],c:0,s:`A growing (expanding) population has a pyramid with a broad base.
So pre-reproductive individuals are more than reproductive ones.`},{id:"neet-2018-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"Which one of the following population interactions is widely used in medical science for the production of antibiotics ?",o:["Commensalism","Mutualism","Parasitism","Amensalism"],c:3,s:`Antibiotics are chemicals made by one microbe that harm another microbe while the producer is unaffected.
This is antibiosis, a form of amensalism.`},{id:"neet-2017-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic growth",q:"Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when :",o:["K < N","The value of 'r' approaches zero","K = N","K > N"],c:2,s:`In logistic growth, dN/dt = rN(K \u2212 N)/K.
When N reaches the carrying capacity K, the factor (K \u2212 N) becomes zero, so growth stops.
The curve then flattens into the asymptote.
So the answer is K = N.`},{id:"neet-2017-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Plant adaptations",q:"Plants which produce characteristic pneumatophores and show vivipary belong to :",o:["Hydrophytes","Mesophytes","Halophytes","Psammophytes"],c:2,s:`Mangrove plants grow in salty, waterlogged soil (halophytes).
They make pneumatophores (breathing roots) to get oxygen.
Their seeds germinate inside the fruit on the plant (vivipary).`},{id:"neet-2017-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"Mycorrhizae are the example of :",o:["Mutualism","Fungistasis","Amensalism","Antibiosis"],c:0,s:`Mycorrhiza is an association of a fungus and plant roots.
The fungus helps absorb water and minerals and the plant gives food.
Both benefit, so this is mutualism.`},{id:"neet-2016-q090",pn:90,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competitive exclusion",q:"Gause's principle of competitive exclusion states that -",o:["Competition for the same resources excludes species having different food preferences","No two species can occupy the same niche indefinitely for the same limiting resources","Larger organisms exclude smaller ones through competition","More abundant species will exclude the less abundant species through competition"],c:1,s:`Gause's principle: two closely related species competing for the same limiting resources cannot coexist indefinitely.
One is eliminated, unless they occupy different niches.`},{id:"neet-2016-q118",pn:118,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic growth",q:"When does the growth rate of a population following the logistic model equal zero? The logistic model is given as dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K) :",o:["When N nears the carrying capacity of the habitat.","When N/K equals zero.","When death rate is greater than birth rate.","When N/K is exactly one."],c:3,s:`dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K).
If N/K = 1 then dN/dt = rN(1 - 1) = 0.
If N/K = 0, N is zero and the growth rate is also zero but the population does not exist; 'nears' K is only approximately zero.
So the exact condition is N/K = 1.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q132",pn:132,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"r- and K-selection",q:"Which of the following is correct for r-selected species ?",o:["Large number of progeny with small size","Large number of progeny with large size","Small number of progeny with small size","Small number of progeny with large size"],c:0,s:`r-selected species have a high rate of increase and put energy into many offspring.
The offspring are small and receive little parental care.
So they produce a large number of progeny of small size.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q133",pn:133,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"If '+' sign is assigned to beneficial interaction, '\u2013' sign to detrimental and '0' sign to neutral interaction, then the population interaction represented by '+' '\u2013' refers to",o:["mutualism","amensalism","commensalism","parasitism"],c:3,s:`Mutualism is (+, +), commensalism is (+, 0) and amensalism is (\u2212, 0).
In parasitism the parasite benefits (+) and the host is harmed (\u2212).
So (+, \u2212) is parasitism (predation also fits this sign).`},{id:"neet-2016b-q177",pn:177,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competitive exclusion",q:"The principle of competitive exclusion was stated by",o:["C. Darwin","G. F. Gause","MacArthur","Verhulst & Pearl"],c:1,s:`Gause's competitive exclusion principle states that two closely related species competing for the same limited resources cannot coexist; the inferior competitor is eliminated.
So the answer is G. F. Gause.`}]})();
